{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Childhood vascular tumors form from cells that make blood vessels or lymph vessels.","source_span":"Childhood vascular tumors form from cells that make blood vessels or lymph vessels."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Childhood vascular tumors may be classified into four groups: benign tumors, intermediate (locally aggressive) tumors, intermediate (rarely metastasizing) tumors, and malignant tumors.","source_span":"Childhood vascular tumors may be classified into four groups.         - Benign tumors     - Intermediate (locally aggressive) tumors     - Intermediate (rarely metastasizing) tumors     - Malignant tumors"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"The most common type of childhood vascular tumor is hemangioma, a benign tumor that usually goes away on its own.","source_span":"The most common type of childhood vascular tumor is hemangioma, which is a benign tumor that usually goes away on its own."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Kaposiform hemangioendothelioma and tufted angioma are classified as intermediate, locally aggressive vascular tumors.","source_span":"This summary has information about the following locally aggressive vascular tumors:            -  Kaposiform hemangioendothelioma and tufted angioma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Epithelioid hemangioendothelioma and angiosarcoma of soft tissue are the malignant vascular tumors covered in the summary.","source_span":"This summary has information about the following malignant vascular tumors:            -  Epithelioid hemangioendothelioma.     -  Angiosarcoma of soft tissue."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Infantile hemangiomas are the most common type of benign vascular tumor in children and may also be called a \"strawberry mark.\"","source_span":"Infantile hemangiomas are the most common type of benign vascular tumor in children. An infantile hemangioma may also be called a \"strawberry mark.\""}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Infantile hemangiomas are not usually seen at birth but appear when the infant is 3 to 6 weeks old.","source_span":"These tumors are not usually seen at birth but appear when the infant is 3 to 6 weeks old."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Most hemangiomas get bigger for about 5 months, then stop growing and slowly fade away completely over the next several years.","source_span":"Most hemangiomas get bigger for about 5 months, then stop growing and slowly fade away completely during the next several years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Infantile hemangiomas are more common in girls, whites, premature babies, twins/triplets/other multiple births, and babies of older mothers or mothers with placental problems.","source_span":"Infantile hemangiomas are more common in the following:          - Girls.    - Whites.    - Premature babies.    -  Twins, triplets, or other multiple births.     - Babies of mothers who are older at time of the pregnancy or who have problems with the placenta during pregnancy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"Having more than five hemangiomas on the skin is a sign there may be hemangiomas in an organ, most commonly the liver.","source_span":"Having more than five hemangiomas on the skin is a sign that there may be hemangiomas in an organ, most commonly the liver."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"PHACE syndrome causes hemangioma to spread across a large area of the body (usually head or face) and may involve other health problems with the large blood vessels, heart, eyes, and/or brain.","source_span":"PHACE syndrome: A syndrome in which the hemangioma spreads across a large area of the body (usually the head or face). Other health problems involving the large blood vessels, heart, eyes, and/or brain may also occur."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","statement":"A physical exam and history are usually all that are needed to diagnose infantile hemangiomas.","source_span":"A physical exam and history are usually all that are needed to diagnose infantile hemangiomas."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is the most common type of childhood vascular tumor, and how does it usually behave over time?","answer":"The most common type is hemangioma, a benign tumor that usually goes away on its own."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Into what four groups are childhood vascular tumors classified?","answer":"They are classified into benign tumors, intermediate (locally aggressive) tumors, intermediate (rarely metastasizing) tumors, and malignant tumors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","fact_refs":[6],"question":"At what age do infantile hemangiomas typically first appear, and are they present at birth?","answer":"They are not usually seen at birth; they typically appear when the infant is 3 to 6 weeks old."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What is the typical growth pattern of infantile hemangiomas after they appear?","answer":"Most hemangiomas get bigger for about 5 months, then stop growing and slowly fade away completely over the next several years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","fact_refs":[10],"question":"Besides the hemangioma itself, what other health problems can occur with PHACE syndrome?","answer":"PHACE syndrome can involve other health problems affecting the large blood vessels, heart, eyes, and/or brain."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_6","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What does having more than five hemangiomas on a child's skin suggest?","answer":"It is a sign that there may be hemangiomas in an organ, most commonly the liver."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"Adult Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system.","source_span":"Adult Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"There are two main types of Hodgkin lymphoma: classical and nodular lymphocyte-predominant.","source_span":"There are two main types of Hodgkin lymphoma: classical and nodular lymphocyte-predominant."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"The classical type of Hodgkin lymphoma is broken into four subtypes: nodular sclerosing, mixed cellularity, lymphocyte depletion, and lymphocyte-rich classical Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"The classical type is broken down into the following four subtypes:         - Nodular sclerosing Hodgkin lymphoma.    - Mixed cellularity Hodgkin lymphoma.    - Lymphocyte depletion Hodgkin lymphoma.    - Lymphocyte-rich classical Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"Risk factors for adult Hodgkin lymphoma include being in young or late adulthood, being male, being infected with Epstein-Barr virus, and having a first-degree relative with Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"Risk factors for adult Hodgkin lymphoma include the following:         - Being in young or late adulthood.     - Being male.     - Being infected with the Epstein-Barr virus.    - Having a first-degree relative (parent, brother, or sister) with Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"Pregnancy is not a risk factor for Hodgkin lymphoma.","source_span":"Pregnancy is not a risk factor for Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"Signs of adult Hodgkin lymphoma include swollen lymph nodes, fever, night sweats, and weight loss.","source_span":"Signs of adult Hodgkin lymphoma include swollen lymph nodes, fever, night sweats, and weight loss."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"Other symptoms checked for in adult Hodgkin lymphoma include itchy skin and feeling very tired.","source_span":"- Itchy skin.    - Feeling very tired."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"The sedimentation rate procedure measures how much inflammation is in the body and is also called erythrocyte sedimentation rate, sed rate, or ESR.","source_span":"The sedimentation rate is a measure of how much inflammation is in the body. A higher than normal sedimentation rate may be a sign of lymphoma or another condition. Also called erythrocyte sedimentation rate, sed rate, or ESR."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"A pathologist views lymph node tissue under a microscope to look for cancer cells, especially Reed-Sternberg cells.","source_span":"A pathologist views the tissue under a microscope to look for cancer cells, especially Reed-Sternberg cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","statement":"Three types of lymph node biopsy are excisional biopsy (removal of an entire lymph node), incisional biopsy (removal of part of a lymph node), and core biopsy (removal of part of a lymph node using a wide needle).","source_span":"-  Excisional biopsy : The removal of an entire lymph node.       -  Incisional biopsy : The removal of part of a lymph node.      -  Core biopsy : The removal of part of a lymph node using a wide needle."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are the two main types of adult Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"The two main types are classical Hodgkin lymphoma and nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What are the four subtypes of classical Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"The four subtypes are nodular sclerosing, mixed cellularity, lymphocyte depletion, and lymphocyte-rich classical Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What risk factors are associated with developing adult Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"Risk factors include being in young or late adulthood, being male, being infected with the Epstein-Barr virus, and having a first-degree relative (parent, brother, or sister) with Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Is pregnancy considered a risk factor for Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"No, pregnancy is not a risk factor for Hodgkin lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"What signs and symptoms are associated with adult Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"Signs include swollen lymph nodes, fever, night sweats, and weight loss, as well as itchy skin and feeling very tired."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What specific type of cell does a pathologist look for under a microscope when diagnosing Hodgkin lymphoma from a biopsy?","answer":"The pathologist looks for cancer cells, especially Reed-Sternberg cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Adult non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system.","source_span":"Adult non-Hodgkin lymphoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the lymph system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma can begin in B lymphocytes, T lymphocytes, or natural killer cells.","source_span":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma can begin in B lymphocytes, T lymphocytes, or natural killer cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Most types of non-Hodgkin lymphoma begin in B lymphocytes.","source_span":"Most types of non-Hodgkin lymphoma begin in B lymphocytes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Indolent lymphoma tends to grow and spread slowly with few signs and symptoms, while aggressive lymphoma grows and spreads quickly with symptoms that can be severe.","source_span":"Indolent lymphoma tends to grow and spread slowly, and has few signs and symptoms. Aggressive lymphoma grows and spreads quickly, and has signs and symptoms that can be severe."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Follicular lymphoma is the most common type of indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma and begins in B lymphocytes.","source_span":"Follicular lymphoma is the most common type of indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma. It is a very slow-growing type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that begins in B lymphocytes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Most patients with follicular lymphoma are age 50 years and older when they are diagnosed.","source_span":"Most patients with follicular lymphoma are age 50 years and older when they are diagnosed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Follicular lymphoma can sometimes become a more aggressive type of lymphoma, such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma.","source_span":"Sometimes follicular lymphoma can become a more aggressive type of lymphoma, such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma is also called Waldenström macroglobulinemia and often spreads to the bone marrow, lymph nodes, and spleen.","source_span":"It is also called Waldenstrm macroglobulinemia... Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma often spreads to the bone marrow, lymph nodes, and spleen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"In lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma, B lymphocytes turning into plasma cells make large amounts of monoclonal immunoglobulin M (IgM) antibody, and high levels of IgM cause the blood plasma to thicken.","source_span":"B lymphocytes that are turning into plasma cells make large amounts of a protein called monoclonal immunoglobulin M (IgM) antibody. High levels of IgM antibody in the blood cause the blood plasma to thicken."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"There are five different types of marginal zone lymphoma, grouped by the type of tissue where the lymphoma formed.","source_span":"There are five different types of marginal zone lymphoma. They are grouped by the type of tissue where the lymphoma formed"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","statement":"Nodal marginal zone lymphoma forms in lymph nodes and is also called monocytoid B-cell lymphoma.","source_span":"Nodal marginal zone lymphoma forms in lymph nodes. This type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma is rare. It is also called monocytoid B-cell lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"In which three types of cells can non-Hodgkin lymphoma begin?","answer":"Non-Hodgkin lymphoma can begin in B lymphocytes, T lymphocytes, or natural killer cells."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is the difference between indolent and aggressive non-Hodgkin lymphoma?","answer":"Indolent lymphoma grows and spreads slowly with few signs and symptoms, while aggressive lymphoma grows and spreads quickly with symptoms that can be severe."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"What is the most common type of indolent non-Hodgkin lymphoma, and what age group is most often diagnosed with it?","answer":"Follicular lymphoma is the most common indolent type, and most patients are age 50 years or older when diagnosed."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What more aggressive lymphoma can follicular lymphoma sometimes transform into?","answer":"Follicular lymphoma can sometimes become a more aggressive type of lymphoma, such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"What is another name for lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma, and what causes the blood plasma to thicken in this disease?","answer":"It is also called Waldenström macroglobulinemia; the plasma thickens because B lymphocytes turning into plasma cells make large amounts of monoclonal immunoglobulin M (IgM) antibody."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000004_2","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How many different types of marginal zone lymphoma are there, and how are they categorized?","answer":"There are five different types of marginal zone lymphoma, grouped by the type of tissue where the lymphoma formed."}
